378 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284337 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186282670 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284337 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186283524 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284337 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284337 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284337 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186283524 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284337 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e4b22f39-03e5-53f5-bf7c-13b93c3d9aae" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284748 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186466669 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186284748 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186466670 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186284748 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186466671 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186284748 #edb/ref 1069 "q-177118" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284748 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284748 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n{u}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,{u}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix},\\,{u}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the vectors above, and let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0$\n- **II.** ${Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2}),Q({u}_{3})}$ is orthogonal\n- **III.** ${Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2}),Q({u}_{3})}$ is orthonormal\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186284748 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1e67e934-1049-5a47-aaca-94c1d2a3bfc1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284748 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is false since\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}=2+12-5=9\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Notice that, for example, $Q({u}_{1})⊥̸Q({u}_{2})$ since\n\n$$\nQ({u}_{1})\\cdot Q({u}_{2})={u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}\\ne 0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false since the set is not even orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186284758 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186284189 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284758 #edb/ref 1069 "q-177810" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284758 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284758 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Find $‖Q(x)‖$, where $x=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -2\\sqrt{3} \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186284758 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "57add274-90e1-53e7-99ed-40cc011b2a2c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186284758 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nThe linear transformation $Q$ being orthogonal is equivalent to the following:\n\n- The matrix $Q$ of transformation $Q$ relative to any orthonormal basis (including the standard basis) of ${R}^{n}$ is orthogonal.\n\n- $||Q(x)||=||x||.$\n\n- If $x⊥y$ then $Q(x)⊥Q(y)$ for any $x,y\\in {R}^{n}.$ In general, $Q$ preserves the angle between any two vectors.\n\n- $Q$ maps any orthogonal (orthonormal) set into an orthogonal (orthonormal) set.\n\nIn this case, since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Q(x)‖ & =‖x‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{(-2\\sqrt{3})}^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324305 #edb/ref 1080 "-4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324305 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324305 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "29e1aa1e-de86-5141-a747-49b7df88362b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324318 #edb/ref 1080 "-3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324318 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186324318 #edb/ref 1083