406653 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "ac075b6f-3299-5310-ad52-26f9cf648385" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406654 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186406651 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406654 #edb/ref 1069 "q-40514" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406654 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406654 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Find $‖Q(x)‖$, where $x=\\begin{bmatrix}\\sqrt{6} \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186406654 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8a69da2c-f673-5e64-a265-9fe904a3c9ad" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406654 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nThe linear transformation $Q$ being orthogonal is equivalent to the following:\n\n- The matrix $Q$ of transformation $Q$ relative to any orthonormal basis (including the standard basis) of ${R}^{n}$ is orthogonal.\n\n- $||Q(x)||=||x||.$\n\n- If $x⊥y$ then $Q(x)⊥Q(y)$ for any $x,y\\in {R}^{n}.$ In general, $Q$ preserves the angle between any two vectors.\n\n- $Q$ maps any orthogonal (orthonormal) set into an orthogonal (orthonormal) set.\n\nIn this case, since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖Q(x)‖ & =‖x‖ \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+{x}_{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\sqrt{6})}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{3}^{2}} \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406655 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186473951 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186406655 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186473952 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186406655 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186473953 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186406655 #edb/ref 1069 "q-40491" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406655 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406655 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Which of the following are orthogonal sets?\n- **U.** $U={Q(\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}),Q(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix})}$\n- **W.** $W={Q(\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}),Q(\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix})}$\n- **V.** $V={Q(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}),Q(\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}),Q(\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix})}$\n\nSelect all that apply." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186406655 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9892c6c7-24de-57f2-a3e8-0b32de0c1349" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406655 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nLet's denote the elements of $U,W,$ and $V$ respectively as \n\n$\\,$ ${Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2})},\\,$ ${Q({w}_{1}),Q({w}_{2})},\\,$ and $\\,{Q({v}_{1}),Q({v}_{2}),Q({v}_{3})}.$ \n\nThe sets $U,V,$ and $W$ are orthogonal if the following conditions on each set hold:\n\n- $Q({u}_{1})\\cdot Q({u}_{2})=0.$\n\n- $Q({w}_{1})\\cdot Q({w}_{2})=0.$\n\n- $Q({v}_{1})\\cdot Q({v}_{2})=0,\\,Q({v}_{1})\\cdot Q({v}_{3})=0,\\,Q({v}_{2})\\cdot Q({v}_{3})=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- For the set $U,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q({u}_{1})\\cdot Q({u}_{2}) & ={u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}9 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}=18-18=0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $U$ is orthogonal.\n\n- For the set $W,$ we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q({w}_{1})\\cdot Q({w}_{2}) & ={w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{6} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}=1-1=0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $W$ is orthogonal.\n\n- For the Set $V,$ notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q({v}_{1})\\cdot Q({v}_{2}) & ={v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}=2+12-5=9\\ne 0.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $V$ is not an orthogonal set.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$U$ and $W$ only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186406657 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186406658 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406657 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0bda9774-6623-5015-9c4f-2876bc204742" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406657 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186406656 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406658 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186406659 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406658 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "5c0620be-2d88-50a9-b295-5b90712b0a8b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406658 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186406655 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406659 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c999b8fd-0d25-5711-adf5-8e3f0c95ef68" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186406659 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186406654 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186408166 #edb/ref 10 :answer-field.type/checkbox #edb/ref 17592186045416] [17592186415149 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186415157 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415149 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186415158 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415149 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186415159 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415149 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186415160 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415149 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186415161 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415149 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415149 #edb/ref 1045 "Orthogonal Linear Transformations" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415149 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186415158 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415149 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046660 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415149 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050910 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415149 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "e4410405-361b-5f52-bbe2-fae1dae52eb7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415156 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186473784 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186415156 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186473785 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186415156 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186473786 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186415156 #edb/ref 1069 "q-178014" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415156 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415156 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n{u}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix},\\,{u}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the vectors above, and let $Q$ be an orthogonal linear transformation. Which of the following statements are true?\n- **I.** ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0$\n- **II.** ${Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2})}$ is orthogonal\n- **III.** ${Q({u}_{1}),Q({u}_{2})}$ is orthonormal\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186415156 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2a7fbace-2464-5d79-be05-e6f28745c3de" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415156 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $Q$ is orthogonal, we have that $Q(x)\\cdot Q(y)=x\\cdot y$ for any vectors $x$ and $y.$\n\nThe linear transformation $Q$ being orthogonal is equivalent to the following:\n\n- The matrix $Q$ of transformation $Q$ relative to any orthonormal basis (including the standard basis) of ${R}^{n}$ is orthogonal.\n\n- $||Q(x)||=||x||.$\n\n- If $x⊥y$ then $Q(x)⊥Q(y)$ for any $x,y\\in {R}^{n}.$ In general, $Q$ preserves the angle between any two vectors.\n\n- $Q$ maps any orthogonal (orthonormal) set into an orthogonal (orthonormal) set.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed,\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -6\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}=12-12=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, $Q({u}_{1})⊥Q({u}_{2})$ since\n\n$$\nQ({u}_{1})\\cdot Q({u}_{2})={u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Notice that, for example, $Q({u}_{1})$ is not a unit vector since\n\n$$\n‖Q({u}_{1})‖=‖{u}_{1}‖=\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+(-6)^{2}}\\ne 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186415157 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186415161 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415157 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2a7a4301-aad4-5d08-a205-223ae4b21b05" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186415157 #edb/ref 1063 #edb